-- EXPECT-ERROR: refinement violation: argument `n` of `bump` does not satisfy precondition `_ > 0`
-- The REMEDY that Task 5's interface-signature warning recommends, proved to
-- be enforced rather than merely accepted.
--
-- `accept/t137` witnesses that a refinement in an `interface` method signature
-- is inert (and now warns), and carries the working `impl`-side spelling
-- alongside it. But t137's own call is a SATISFYING one, so it exits 0 whether
-- the impl-parameter refinement is enforced or silently ignored -- an
-- accept-only witness cannot tell a working contract from one that checks
-- nothing, which is the exact trap this corpus has been bitten by before
-- (see the `List.length` alias, `accept/t118` / `reject/t117`).
--
-- This file is the reject companion: the same `Bumper`/`Box` shape as t137's
-- second half, with a VIOLATING call. If `adoptable_impl_methods` ever stopped
-- adopting impl-method parameter refinements, t137 would still exit 0 and the
-- `interface-signature-refinement` alcotest suite would still be green -- the
-- remedy the warning tells authors to use would have silently stopped working
-- with nothing failing. This witness is what fails in that case.
--
-- `bump` is adoptable here: exactly one `impl` defines it and no top-level
-- `fn` shares the name. That condition is load-bearing and is stated in the
-- warning text itself -- with a second `impl Bumper(Crate)` also defining
-- `bump`, the refinement is NOT adopted and this same call exits 0.
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md, "A refinement in an interface signature
-- enforces nothing".
mod T138 do

  type Box = Box(Int)

  interface Bumper(a) do
    fn bump : a -> Int -> Int
  end

  impl Bumper(Box) do
    fn bump(_b : Box, n : {Int | _ > 0}) : Int do n end
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    bump(Box(1), 0 - 5)
  end

end
